    "body": "AI-generated image\nBy Alex Lennane\n26 November 2026\nIs it the beginning of the end for airfreight’s new best friend? Following new US export controls, Nvidia has reportedly told its manufacturing partners to stop shipping servers containing its chips to Malaysia, Singapore, Thailand, and Indonesia.\nAccording to the UK Financial Times today, citing three sources, manufacturers including Dell and Supermicro have told buyers in the four countries that Nvidia will not allow their orders to ship.\nIt was unclear how long the halt would last – none of the companies has issued a comment.\nNikkei Asia had already reported that at least two Nvidia server partners had suspended shipments to South-east Asian customers, with one also pausing orders from new customers in the region. The reports did not quantify the cargo affected or identify which shipments had been booked to fly.\nThe uncertainty is also affecting financing. Bloomberg reported that banks had paused a $480m loan to fund purchases of Nvidia GPUs for a data centre in Malaysia.\nSince 19 November, the US has required licences for shipments of certain advanced chips subject to its export controls to those four South-east Asian countries and Brazil. The rules can cover some foreign-made chips and include an exclusion for qualifying US-headquartered end users or consignees, or those with a US-headquartered ultimate parent.\nSeparate provisions target overseas data centres supplying significant computing power to certain restricted users.\nWashington also moved this week to add seven companies to its Entity List, including those linked to Nvidia partner Megaspeed, citing the risk of diversion. Malaysia said it would advise its data centre operators against hosting or providing services to the listed companies.\nThe US move came on the day Nvidia announced Jensen Huang’s return as chief executive. He is due to testify before the US Senate Banking Committee on export controls on 3 December.\nThe reported shipment halt comes amid strong demand for air cargo within Asia. Fred Ruggiero, Cathay Cargo’s Americas cargo chief, told The Loadstar at TIACA’s Air Cargo Forum in Miami that the carrier was looking for more capacity within the region. Another airline executive at the forum pointed to technology shipments from Taiwan and Vietnam as a major source of pressure on space.\nCathay, for example, carried 162,747 tonnes in October, up 7.5% year on year, while its network-wide cargo capacity rose just 0.5%. It cited strong export demand from North-east and South-east Asia, including shipments of network equipment from the south-east, and said semiconductor traffic helped drive growth in its specialist cargo business. An additional A330 freighter operated by Air Hong Kong, under a lease announced in June, is due to serve selected regional routes. Across the industry, IATA recorded a 10.2% year-on-year rise in air cargo traffic within Asia in September.\nMeanwhile, South Korea’s semiconductor exports nearly quadrupled in value, year on year, in the first 20 days of November, rising 294.4%, to $38.5bn. Chips accounted for 49.7% of the country’s export value over the period.\nHowever, the traffic is not just one-way; Cathay is carrying server racks in both directions across the Pacific.\n“We ship a good number of server racks from Asia into the US, as well as from the US to Asia,” said US-based Mr Ruggiero, although he admitted that “we ship more on the inbound than outbound”.\nThe rush to build data centres is also putting some of the equipment that powers them on aircraft. A GE Vernova logistics representative told the Miami forum conventional utility projects typically ran on a multi-year timetable. Hyperscalers gave the company less time to manufacture and deliver equipment to site, she said, so it used “a lot more air freight”.\nThe air cargo industry will be hoping that these suspensions are just that – suspensions – and that all the AI hardware will soon take to the skies again. Preferably, in time to boost what would otherwise undoubtedly be a lacklustre January.",
